const BATCH_DESKTOP = COMMON + `
YOUR BATCH: the desktop as a tmux client, retrospective finding 8 (its clipboard half), finding 14, and the harness lens's "overlay states without a consumer", three groups. BRANCHNAME: batch-desktop-tmux-client. WORKDIR: zz-lane-desktop (fresh at origin/main, COLD target: the first cargo build compiles every dependency, gpui, CEF bindings and libghostty included; budget 25-35 minutes under the shared load and do not mistake it for a hang; start cargo build -p zz -p zz-tui --jobs ${M.workerJobs} > build.log 2>&1 FIRST, in the foreground, before you read code, and check its exit code).
Your zones: crates/zz/src/ EXCEPT control_mode.rs and EXCEPT lib.rs (both belong to the daemon lane this cycle; touch lib.rs nowhere); crates/zz-client/src/; crates/zz-tui/src/. The daemon lane owns crates/zz-daemon, crates/zz-mux and control_mode.rs; the proof lane owns compat/ and knowledge/ except the fixtures and pages your own groups need (name every compat/ and knowledge/ file you touch in notes). compat/tmux-gaps.json and compat/results/summary.md are shared: add your own new groups and rows; never edit the four open groups (${OPEN_GROUPS}), sessions.linked-groups, or any accepted group's record except to relocate a slug you prove (say so in notes).
PROTOCOL: no bump expected. Everything here is client-side rendering, input and clipboard.

1. desktop.drag-to-clipboard, NEW OPEN GROUP. HARD BUDGET 60 MINUTES. Finding 8's clipboard half: on Linux a drag selection in the desktop lands in PRIMARY only (crates/zz/src/mux/client.rs, the CoreEvent::Clipboard arm: ClipboardTarget::Primary writes primary on linux/freebsd and the clipboard elsewhere), so a tmux-yank user who drag-selects and pastes with Ctrl+V gets nothing. Measure the pin first with an attached client: with set -g mouse on and the default bindings a MouseDragEnd1Pane runs copy-selection-and-cancel, and with set-clipboard at its default (external) the pin writes the selection through OSC 52 to the outer terminal (read tty.c tty_set_selection and window-copy.c for the exact selection target and the set-clipboard gating); tmux-yank (compat/.cache/plugins/tmux-yank/yank.tmux) binds MouseDragEnd1Pane copy-pipe-and-cancel with the clipboard command. DECISION (record it as the registry rule says, with "${DECIDED}"): on Linux the desktop mirrors a drag selection to CLIPBOARD in addition to PRIMARY whenever the session's set-clipboard option is not off, and writes PRIMARY only when it is off; macOS is unchanged (one pasteboard). Items: semantic:desktop-drag-selection-clipboard. Proof: the GPUI half is a #[gpui::test] in crates/zz that drives a drag selection and asserts the clipboard writes per target and per set-clipboard value; the raw TUI half is measured on both binaries through the OSC 52 path (crates/zz-tui/src/clipboard.rs) with an attached pty fixture that captures the OSC 52 bytes each side emits after a copy-selection with set-clipboard on and off; parity there, or the divergence recorded with the bytes. Say in notes which half is unit-proved.
2. desktop.overlay-consumers, NEW OPEN GROUP. HARD BUDGET 90 MINUTES. The harness lens's finding: the GPUI client was never run under the campaign, and daemon overlay state can exist with no desktop consumer (cycle 11's silent destructive kill in the desktop chooser was that class). Build a #[gpui::test] matrix in crates/zz that feeds EVERY overlay payload kind the daemon can publish (crates/zz-protocol/src/message.rs: ChooseTree, ChooseTreeUpdate, ChooseBuffer, ChooseBufferUpdate, DisplayPanes, CommandPrompt, Popup, Menu, Confirm, the Overlay enum near line 1878, and any kind you find that this list does not name) into the workspace through the same reduction path the live client uses (zz_client::ClientCore -> CoreEvent -> the GPUI MuxClient), and asserts for each kind that a consumer exists: an element renders, a state transition happens, or an action reaches the daemon. A kind that falls into a no-op arm is a finding: record it as its own item with what the raw TUI does with the same payload, and fix it inside the budget when the fix is a wiring. Items: presentation:gui-overlay-consumer-matrix, plus one presentation:gui-overlay-<kind>-unconsumed per kind you find unconsumed and cannot fix within budget. Proof: cargo test -p zz (headless gpui tests) green at tip.
3. tui.sidebar-auto-hide, NEW OPEN GROUP. HARD BUDGET 45 MINUTES. Finding 14: the raw TUI at 80 columns keeps its sidebar (crates/zz-tui/src/sidebar.rs: WIDTH 28, BORDER_WIDTH 1, AUTO_HIDE_COLUMNS 80 with Visibility::Auto => columns >= AUTO_HIDE_COLUMNS, STATUS_ROWS 3), so 'ssh -t host zz attach' on an 80x24 terminal gets about a 51x21 pane where tmux gives 80x23. DECISION (record it with "${DECIDED}"): at 80 columns the sidebar is hidden by default; pick the threshold so an 80-column terminal gets the full width, measure what 100 and 120 columns get, and write the constant and the rationale into the reason. Rows are NOT in this budget: measure zz's row count at 80x24 and record it honestly beside the pin's 23. Items: presentation:tui-sidebar-auto-hide-80. Proof: a fixture in the shape of compat/status-row.sh (both binaries attached inside an outer pinned tmux sized 80x24) that runs 'tput cols; tput lines' inside the pane on both sides and diffs the columns; the rows line is recorded, not asserted equal.
`

